TASK 2: GIT REVERT— HANDS-ON

Create 3 commits

touch revert.txt

echo "Commit X" > revert.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Commit X"

echo "Commit Y" >> revert.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Commit Y"

echo "Commit Z" >> revert.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Commit Z"

Check history:

git log --oneline

Copy the commit ID of Commit Y and run:

git revert <commit-id>

Example:

git revert a1b2c3d

Check history again:

git log --oneline

Observation

- Git creates a new commit that reverses Commit Y.
- Commit Y is still present in history.
- History is not rewritten.



Git Reset vs Git Revert

Feature| git reset| git revert
What it does| Removes commit| Creates a new commit to undo changes
Removes commit history| Yes| No
Safe for pushed/shared branches| No| Yes
Best used for| Local commits| Shared or pushed commits

-

Why is Revert Safer?

- Does not rewrite Git history.
- Safe for team projects.
- Recommended for "main" or shared branches.

-

Git Reflog (Safety Net)

git reflog

Shows all recent Git actions, even after a hard reset.

Recover a lost commit:

git reset --hard HEAD@{1}

